# todo/t0953 — a closure LITERAL at a call-argument position mints a heap
# environment that is never freed after the call returns.
#
# Two cells, because the class is WIDER than the characterisation
# `tests/sanitize/LEAK_ALLOWLIST.txt` inherited for it ("every builtin HOF call
# (map/filter/fold) leaks an 8-byte env"):
#
#   A  builtin Vector HOF   `nums.map((int n): n + base)`
#   B  a plain USER function taking `Callable[int(int)]`
#
# B is the discriminator: it has no builtin HOF anywhere, and it leaks the same
# frame. So the write site is the closure-literal ARGUMENT, not the HOF.
#
# ⭐ CELL A IS CLOSED, CELL B IS NOT (R50 Track E). The three builtin-HOF
# expanders now drain the closure-argument temp they mint, so cell A is clean.
# Cell B is deliberately NOT drained: a plain callee is USER code that may
# RETAIN a borrowed `Callable` — `lib/std/iter.gg`'s six lazy adapters do — and
# freeing the caller's temp there makes a SECOND owner. Measured, the naive
# drain turned four corpus fixtures into a use-after-free and a double-free and
# moved `scripts/sanitize_sweep.sh` from `corruption: 1` to `corruption: 5`.
# The blocking half is `todo/t1349`; its write site is `todo/t1350`.
#
# HEAP-FORCED: both closures CAPTURE `base`, so each env is a real 16-byte malloc
# rather than the 8-byte placeholder a non-capturing closure gets.
#
# INTENDED: prints 41 and 41, and is ASan-clean under `detect_leaks=1`, on C and
# LLVM. At HEAD it prints both correctly and leaks **16 bytes in 1 allocation**
# — cell B only, since R50 Track E drained cell A; it was 32 bytes in 2.
# Stdout-invisible, so this is wired to `assert_gg_sanitize_clean`. Regenerate
# either figure with `gg build --sanitize` on this file under
# `ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1:exitcode=0 LSAN_OPTIONS=use_stacks=0`.

int apply(Callable[int(int)] f, int x):
    return f(x)

void main():
    int base = 40
    Vector[int] nums = [1, 2, 3]

    # A — builtin HOF.
    auto out = nums.map((int n): n + base)
    print(f"{out.get(0).unwrap()}")

    # B — a plain user function. No HOF, same leak.
    print(f"{apply((int n): n + base, 1)}")
